DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-00232-7_8
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105028272352?origin=resultslist
AB  - The application of artificial intelligence (AI) in cybersecurity has revolutionized the way organizations respond to dynamic threats. AI technologies enable real-time analysis of large datasets to detect potential breaches and enhance response systems. The intelligence-based mechanism not only automates security processes but also enhances human capabilities, fostering greater synergy between AI and cybersecurity professionals. AI’s ability to continuously monitor user Behaviour and system vulnerabilities facilitates proactive risk management, emphasizing preventive over reactive measures. Given these advancements, the objective of this paper is to provide a comprehensive overview of AI in cybersecurity, examining its transformative impact while addressing the challenges associated with its implementation. By employing machine learning (ML), AI tools can learn from historical attack patterns, improving detection rates and ensuring defenses remain robust against emerging threats. However, despite its advantages, AI in cybersecurity presents challenges, including ethical concerns related to privacy, accountability, and potential misuse in cybercrime. These issues necessitate ongoing human oversight and the establishment of strong ethical frameworks. Through an analysis of AI’s ability to learn and adapt, this research underscores its critical role in combating sophisticated cyberattacks and strengthening the overall security landscape. Future work will prioritize autonomous intrusion detection, Behaviour-based analytics using deep learning, and Explainable AI (XAI) to enhance transparency and trust. The use of Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) Networks and transformer models will support proactive threat hunting, while federated and privacy-preserving AI will enable secure, regulation-compliant systems. To support this, the paper recommends clear AI testing standards, transparency requirements, ethical research incentives, and stronger public-private collaboration on AI auditing and bias mitigation. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2026.
