TI  - Simulating Emergency Teamwork Behavior with Multi-Agent LLMs: Reproducing Human Training Patterns Using Persona-Based Agents
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of 2nd International Conference on Multi-Agent Systems for Collaborative Intelligence, ICMSCI 2026
SP  - 201
EP  - 208
DO  - 10.1109/ICMSCI67830.2026.11469339
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037903853?origin=resultslist
AB  - Emergency response teams such as Computer Security Incident Response Teams (CSIRTs) must operate under intense time pressure, rapidly evolving threats, and incomplete information. Team effectiveness in these environments depends strongly on the interaction among individual skill proficiency, leadership style, and collective coordination. Traditional human emergency training exercises are costly, difficult to repeat under controlled conditions, and limited in the diversity of team configurations that can be systematically examined. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and multi-agent conversational frameworks raise a new question: Can LLM-driven multi-agent simulations reproduce human teamwork behavior observed in real emergency training? This study investigates the extent to which persona-based LLM agents can replicate behavioral patterns documented in a publicly available emergency response training dataset. We construct triad teams of LLM agents, assign personas reflecting human skill and leadership profiles, and expose them to an incident scenario parallel to that used in the human exercises. Each conversation log is evaluated using the same 11-item teamwork behavior rubric applied in the human study. Experimental results show that approximately 60% of the behavioral tendencies observed in human teams-such as skillbalanced coordination or follower-dominant communication - are reproduced by the LLM multi-agent simulations. These findings provide the first empirical evidence that LLM-based multi-agent simulations can partially approximate human teamwork behavior in emergency contexts. The proposed approach offers a low-cost and reproducible framework for analyzing team dynamics, exploring hypothetical team structures, and supporting digital-twin-based training tools for CSIRT and other emergency response organizations.  © 2026 IEEE.
