TI  - Secure-by-Design Framework for Agentic AI: Mitigating Toxic Flows and Adversarial Exploits in Multi-Agent Ecosystems
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 Intermountain Engineering, Technology and Computing, IETC 2026
DO  - 10.1109/IETC69527.2026.11568651
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044084777?origin=resultslist
AB  - Large Language Model (LLM)-driven autonomous agents leveraging the Model Context Protocol (MCP) for tool interaction face a critical vulnerability class we term "Toxic Flows."These attacks propagate malicious instructions through untrusted data sources to compromise agent decision-making, enabling unauthorized actions and data exfiltration. We present a Secure-by-Design (SbD) framework centered on Toxic Flow Analysis (TFA), adapting source-to-sink reachability concepts to agentic workflows where non-deterministic LLM behavior precludes traditional dataflow assumptions. Our formal model employs a product lattice decomposing tool capabilities into orthogonal confidentiality, integrity, and side-effect dimensions, with explicit soundness properties under stated assumptions. We formalize trust propagation through information-flow lattices, introduce verifiable sanitizer specifications for constrained output domains, and handle cyclic agent loops through monotonic fixed-point iteration over the full lattice structure. We implement TFA as a Python framework and evaluate it on a synthetic benchmark (500 graphs, seed=42). Results demonstrate 94.2% attack detection with 3.1% false positive rate and 47ms average latency. © 2026 IEEE.
