AB  - Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) are essential for defending against increasingly sophisticated cyber threats, yet many deep learning approaches remain difficult to interpret and depend on fixed attack labels. This paper presents a multi-stage framework that combines a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) for binary traffic classification, Local Interpretable Model-agnostic Explanations (LIME) for instance-level explanations, and a large language model (LLM)-based agent for analyst-facing interpretation. The CNN is trained only for benign-versus-attack discrimination, thereby avoiding per-attack-type supervision at the detector stage, after which LIME highlights the most influential features for each decision. An LLM agent then converts these feature attributions and the CNN’s binary prediction into concise natural-language justifications and, when the evidence is sufficiently distinctive, proposes a likely attack family for analyst review. Experiments on the CSE-CIC-IDS2018 benchmark show that the CNN attains 94.94% accuracy and a 94.69% F1-score on the balanced test split used in this study, while the LLM agent reaches 97% agreement with ground-truth labels (95% Wilson confidence interval: 91.5–99.0%). The results indicate that coupling local XAI with LLM-based interpretation can improve the readability and auditability of NIDS outputs. The current framework is intended as a post-hoc analyst-support layer rather than a strict real-time inline defense, and broader validation under operational traffic distributions remains future work. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.
