TI  - An Intelligent Large Language Model Driven Neurosymbolic Architecture for Autonomous Cyber Threat Identification and Containment
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Sustainable Computing and Integrated Communication in Changing Landscape of Al, ICSCAI 2026
SP  - 250
EP  - 256
DO  - 10.1109/ICSCAI68849.2026.11648650
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048714072?origin=resultslist
AB  - The fast-growing capabilities of cyber threats that include automated malware, AI-produced exploits, and multi-stage intrusions organized by coordinating defense measures have rendered traditional defense systems less effective. Current security designs are largely and mostly detection based and rely on human observers to interpret, give threat prioritization, and execution of responses. This kind of manual intervention creates some delays that can be used by opponents to increase privileges, side-step, or install persistence. In this study, a smart LLM-based neurosymbolic architecture to identify and contain autonomously cyber threats will be proposed, which can think in real-time and context using intelligent reasoning and a micro-level level of response. This model combines three new technologies: (1) Threat Capsule Generator: compact, high-signal representation units produced by the transformation has to be transduced into raw logs; (2) Hybrid NeuralSymbolic Reasoning Loop: the deep contextualised understanding is to be balanced with the deterministic assurance of policy; and (3) Dynamic Isolation Mesh: micro-segments containment domains are to be created in mainland to restrict the spread of malicious processes without interrupting normal business activity. The architecture also includes an Explainability Layer which provides autonomous integrity of the system and a Self-Healing Module which provides transparent auditability and trust. There are experimental assessments of the proposed system with real and synthetic attack data which prove that their system causes a reduction in containment latency by 70 percent, as well as increases the zero-day detection rates by 40 percent and sustains greater than 98 percent system uptime in the active mitigation conditions. The findings affirm the viability and effectiveness of autonomous containment with the help of the LLM, which is an important step in the next-generation cyber defence. © 2026 IEEE.
