IS  - 2026
SP  - 332
EP  - 338
DO  - 10.1109/ICCCI70321.2026.11666544
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049802103?origin=resultslist
AB  - Traditional vulnerability response systems frequently rely on isolated vulnerability metrics, such as the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS), to prioritize remediation efforts. However, these static scores fail to capture the cascading effects of attack propagation, lateral movement, and asset interdependencies, leading to inaccurate risk assessment in complex network environments. This paper proposes GAI-AVRS, an autonomous vulnerability response system that integrates generative AI, an Edge-aware Graph Attention Network (Edgeaware GAT), and Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG). GAI-AVRS constructs heterogeneous attack graphs from assets, vulnerabilities, components, and network connections; learns node and edge level risk representations; computes path level risk scores; and aggregates them into global vulnerability level remediation priorities. In the response stage, Large Language Models (LLMs) leverage Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE) details and Model Context Protocol (MCP) Server/Tool retrieval results to generate mitigation reports with remediation order and mitigation procedures. Experiments on a MITRE ATT&CK informed dataset show that GAI-AVRS outperforms CVSS, Exploit Prediction Scoring System (EPSS), GPT based ranking, and random baselines in path-level and vulnerability level NDCG@K. For mitigation report generation, GPT-5.5 achieves the best overall quality in Precision, Recall, Embedding Cosine Similarity, and Answer Correctness.  © 2026 IEEE.
