SP  - 71
EP  - 76
DO  - 10.1109/INSPIRE67328.2025.11300587
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105032481041?origin=resultslist
AB  - Security Operations Centers (SOCs) are confronted with the growing threats posed by advanced persistent threats (APTs), zero-day attacks, and multi-stage attacks that bypass rule-based detection. The available tools are short on semantic correlation, proactive prediction, and explainability, causing high false positives and latency in response. To mitigate such challenges, this paper introduces the Cognitive Retrieval, Reasoning, and Threat Expansion (CRRTE) framework, an autonomous threat hunting system driven by Large Language Models (LLMs). CRRTE combines FAISS-based multi-vector retrieval with MITRE ATT&CK, CVE, and CWE ontologies for real-time correlation, utilizes graph reasoning to reconstruct broken attack paths, and uses semantic expansion for predictive threat evolution. Explainability is realized through SHAP-driven interpretability, while analyst feedback guarantees adaptability. Experimental verification on the CSE-CIC-IDS 2018 dataset, enriched with simulated APTs, shows 94.3% detection accuracy, 96.2% retrieval accuracy, 91.6% prediction precision, and 93.5% threat expansion accuracy, with an AUC of 0.965 in ROC analysis. False positives were decreased by 38% and 92% analyst agreement was achieved with the framework. Objective-to-result mapping verifies that CRRTE effectively maps real-time detection, semantic correlation, predictive reasoning, explainability, and adaptability with its design objectives. In contrast to THAD-AI and RAG-GPT, CRRTE provides better performance, scalability, and interpretability, making it a robust cognitive cyber defense framework for SOC setups. © 2025 IEEE.
