TI  - Cybersecurity Threat Analysis utilizing Deep Learning: Apply Natural Language Processing Techniques to Extract Compromise Indicators from Unstructured Logs
PY  - 2025
T2  - Proceedings - IEEE 10th International Conference on Smart Structures and Systems, ICSSS 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICSSS66939.2025.11346164
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033145134?origin=resultslist
AB  - Abstract-The methods that hackers use to break into systems are becoming increasingly sophisticated. Cyber security systems of today are required to deal with a large amount of unstructured data, which includes system logs, network traffic, and threat intelligence reports, in order to find possible indicators of compromise (IoCs). Because of the many, complicated, and confusing cyber incidents that have happened in the last few years, traditional rule-based systems and manual log analysis techniques are no longer enough. This study aims to illustrate the application of an advanced deep learning architecture and Natural Language Processing (NLP) techniques to autonomously identify, interpret, and correlate indicators of compromise within unstructured cybersecurity data. The method uses transformer-based designs like BERT and GPT to figure out what words imply based on the context in which they are used. Because of this, the system can find behaviors that weren't expected, threat patterns that were hidden, and attack signatures that had never been seen before. The recommended solution uses preprocessing pipelines to do both log normalization and entity recognition. Then, it uses semantic embedding to turn unstructured log entries into vectors that the learning model can understand. It is possible for the model to detect critical signals such as IP anomalies, unlawful access attempts, and hazardous payloads without the requirement to be configured with specific criteria. The labeled threat datasets not only contribute to the further improvement of the deep learning module, but it also makes use of attention techniques in order to select the most significant tokens associated with threats. The results of experimental assessments show that the accuracy and recall of IoC extraction are significantly higher than those of typical machine learning benchmarks. Additionally, it is able to manage a wide variety of log formats and an everevolving threat vocabulary. The goal of this project is to construct an autonomous threat analysis system that is able to learn from data streams that are constantly changing. This project links natural language processing (NLP) and cybersecurity. Furthermore, it improves the effectiveness of proactive security methods and makes it simpler for analysts to identify items in real time. According to the findings of our research, powerful natural language processing (NLP)driven cybersecurity analytics have the potential to transform threat intelligence by transforming unstructured data into a dependable and efficient method of identifying and resolving new cyber threats.  © 2025 IEEE.
