TI  - SAMSARA: Architecture-aware LLM-Agentic Vulnerability Detection for Microservices
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the International Conference on Software Engineering and Knowledge Engineering, SEKE
SP  - 303
EP  - 308
DO  - 10.18293/SEKE2026-081
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050542517?origin=resultslist
AB  - Many security failures in microservice systems arise not from bugs within a single service but from mismatches across services: authorization enforced upstream but skipped downstream, data crossing a service boundary without re-validation, or services with incompatible assumptions about shared storage. Pattern-based SAST analyzes code in isolation and misses these failures; prior LLM detectors focus on single projects, and static cross-service analyzers do not reason about authorization intent. This paper introduces SAMSARA, a framework that models a target codebase as a microservice system M = (S,E,D) of services, communication edges, and shared dependencies, and uses LLM agents augmented with domain-specific knowledge to detect cross-service vulnerabilities including BOLA, BFLA, and cross-service taint flows. SAMSARA runs in three phases: service-map construction, orchestrator-worker analysis with parallel Verification Agents followed by an independent Judge Agent, and iterative remediation. We implement and evaluate SAMSARA on six benchmarks: its recall margin over Semgrep is small on single-file tests (+5.8%) but large on cross-service systems (+61-85%). Component ablations show the service map drives precision (removal nearly doubles reported findings), iteration improves recall and precision, and skills shift coverage. © 2026 Knowledge Systems Institute Graduate School. All rights reserved.
