TI  - Self-Auditing Large Language Models for Real-Time Intrusion Prediction in Encrypted Networks
PY  - 2025
T2  - 3rd International Conference on Emerging Research in Computational Science, ICERCS 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICERCS65898.2025.11580588
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044504727?origin=resultslist
AB  - Encrypted traffic increasingly limits signature-based intrusion detection, creating a critical gap in real-time threat prediction for modern high-throughput networks. This study introduces a self-auditing large language model (SA-LLM) capable of autonomous consistency checking, temporal reasoning, and uncertainty-aware inference without decrypting payloads. The model processes flow-level statistical descriptors, TLS handshake metadata, and sequence-aligned autoencoder embeddings to generate sub-millisecond intrusion predictions. A dual-loop auditing mechanism dynamically recalibrates thresholds under load based on token uncertainty and reconstruction divergence. Experiments were conducted using CIC-Darknet2020, USTC-TFC2018, and a proprietary TLS dataset spanning 52 encrypted attack classes. SA-LLM achieved 96.8% accuracy, a 37% reduction in false positives, and a 4.2× throughput improvement over transformer baselines. Practical testing was performed in Visual Studio Code on a standard laptop (Intel i7-12700H CPU, 16 GB RAM, RTX 3050 GPU), confirming stable inference performance at 9.6k flows/s in local mode with only 14% GPU utilization. Distributed edge deployment further scaled throughput to 18.4k flows/s with minimal overhead. These results validate the SA-LLM as a privacy-preserving, encryption-agnostic, and computationally efficient solution for real-time intrusion prediction across enterprise, industrial, and cloud infrastructures.  © 2025 IEEE.
