TI  - An Adaptive Edge-AI-Enabled Ionizing Radiation Sensing Architecture for Distributed Nanosatellite and Autonomous Micro-Platform Systems
PY  - 2026
T2  - 7th International Conference on Inventive Research in Computing Applications, ICIRCA 2026 - Proceedings
SP  - 715
EP  - 720
DO  - 10.1109/ICIRCA69024.2026.11570308
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044264211?origin=resultslist
AB  - The widespread deployment of nanosatellites, micro-drones, autonomous vehicles and beyond-5G/6G wireless networks requires efficient, scalable, and energy-aware monitoring and resource allocation. Conventional systems based on a centralised architecture have high latency, communication overhead, low scalability and low adaptability. To overcome these limitations, this study introduces an Edge-AI-powered Federated Deep Reinforcement Learning (FDRL) approach for real-time monitoring of radiation and energy-efficient wireless resource allocation. The proposed framework adopts a decentralized approach by combining distributed ionizing radiation monitoring sensors, edge computing platforms, federated learning, lightweight deep neural networks, and reinforcement learning for local intelligence and autonomous learning. A layered edge computing approach enables local data processing with global context awareness through federated learning. The spectrum and environment monitoring problems are redefined using Markov Decision Process (MDP)-based optimization to provide a tradeoff between detection accuracy, spectral efficiency, energy efficiency and Quality of Service (QoS). The framework reduces communication overhead, ensures privacy, and improves scalability in dynamic environments. Experimental and simulation results show substantial performance gains with spectral efficiency of 9.1 bps/Hz, energy efficiency of 91.3%, throughput of 195 Mbps, and QoS satisfaction of 96.8% while latency is reduced from 15 ms to 8 ms and communication overhead from 18% to 8%. Scalability tests also verify the stable convergence and improved training accuracy with the increasing number of distributed edge nodes. The use of federated intelligence and edge AI allows for efficient real-time anomaly detection, resource allocation, and autonomous fault-tolerant operation on diverse platforms. In summary, the proposed framework offers a scalable, privacy-preserving and intelligent approach for future space systems, critical environment monitoring, and beyond 5G/6G wireless communication networks. © 2026 IEEE.
