PY  - 2026
T2  - Annual IEEE Communications Society Conference on Sensor, Mesh and Ad Hoc Communications and Networks workshops
SP  - 524
EP  - 532
DO  - 10.1109/SECON68281.2026.11578985
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044561317?origin=resultslist
AB  - Online advertisements (ads) play a critical role in sustaining the mobile ecosystem. However, imperfect ad review mechanisms allow ads to be exploited as a channel for malicious content dissemination, posing significant security risks to users and platforms. Existing malicious advertisement detection methods heavily rely on manually crafted rules, which limit automation and undermine detection robustness in complex and dynamic environments. To address these limitations, this paper proposes AdSeeker, an automated malicious ad detection framework driven by an autonomous agent. AdSeeker leverages the agent's decision-making capability to systematically explore application interfaces and collect multi-dimensional ad-related evidence, including ad SDK metadata, permission usage, network redirection links, and ad image elements. Based on the collected evidence, AdSeeker classifies malicious ad behaviors, assesses risk levels, and provides remediation suggestions, enabling quantitative security evaluation of mobile applications. We evaluate AdSeeker on 30,000 applications from AndroZoo. The results show that 10,497 apps contain ads, from which 14,971 ad samples are collected, including 1,537 malicious ads. AdSeeker achieves a detection precision of 96.30%. Our findings further reveal that malicious ads are present even in legitimate applications, highlighting the necessity for strengthened ad network auditing and more effective malicious ad governance to foster a safer mobile ecosystem. The source code is publicly available on GitHub at https://github.com/AnaY1115/AdSeeker.  © 2026 IEEE.
