AU  - Arslan, R.
AU  - Özseven, T.
TI  - Mapping the Research Landscape of Large Language Models in Network Intrusion Detection
PY  - 2026
T2  - ISADES 2026 - 2nd International Symposium on AI-Driven Engineering Systems, Proceedings
DO  - 10.1109/ISADES69945.2026.11608180
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046119614?origin=resultslist
AB  - Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS) remain a critical component of modern cybersecurity infrastructure; however, conventional signature- and rule-based approaches are increasingly challenged by novel, polymorphic, and rapidly evolving attack patterns. In this context, large language models (LLMs), transformer architectures, and generative AI approaches have attracted increasing attention as potential tools for analysing network traffic, security logs, and cyber threat patterns. This study presents a PRISMA 2020-guided bibliometric analysis of LLM- and transformer-based NIDS research published between 2010 and 2025. A comprehensive Boolean search strategy was applied to the Web of Science Core Collection and Scopus databases, yielding 11,381 records. After DOI- and title-based duplicate removal, document type, year, language screening, and dual-condition topic-eligibility assessment, a final analytical corpus of 814 peer-reviewed journal articles was retained. The corpus was analysed using the R bibliometrix package and VOSviewer through performance analysis, citation and co-citation analysis, Bradford's Law, Lotka's Law, keyword co-occurrence, reference publication year spectroscopy and thematic mapping. The findings show that research activity remained marginal until 2020, with only 11 articles before 2021, but expanded sharply after the emergence and diffusion of transformer-based security applications, reaching 578 articles in 2025, representing 71.0% of the corpus. The long-term compound annual growth rate was 69.9% for 2013-2025, whereas the post-inflection growth rate reached 213.3% for 2021-2025. China was the leading contributor, with 374 articles (45.9%), followed by India and the United States, while IEEE Access was the most productive source, with 102 articles (12.5%). Cocitation and keyword analyses indicate that Transformer- and BERT-related foundations constitute the intellectual core of the field, whereas XAI, federated learning, and autonomous security agents remain underrepresented but strategically important research directions. Overall, this study provides an evidence-based map of the publication dynamics, intellectual structure, and emerging priorities of network intrusion detection research based on LLMs and transformers. © 2026 IEEE.
