AU  - Gudur, V.
AU  - Ramavath, S.K.
TI  - Leveraging Generative AI And Reinforcement Learning For Autonomous Cyber Threat Mitigation
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 IEEE 5th International Conference on AI in Cybersecurity, ICAIC 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICAIC67076.2026.11395822
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041765531?origin=resultslist
AB  - Cybersecurity is up against attackers that are getting smarter and faster than traditional defenses. This paper proposes a new AI-driven cybersecurity framework that leverages machine learning (ML) and generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) to enhance threat detection, automate incident response, improve anomaly detection, and bolster zero-day exploit prevention. The framework solves some of the greatest concerns with AI-based security systems today, such as too many false positives, difficulty detecting new attacks, sluggish response times, and difficulty integrating. It does this by employing a structure that blends generative models (such GANs and VAEs), sophisticated deep learning, and large language model (LLM) methods. We explain how synthetic data generation and generative modeling help find unusual behavior and unknown threats before they happen, and how reinforcement learning and automation make it easier to take quick action to contain them. A proof-of-concept simulation shows that the framework could greatly improve the accuracy of detection and the speed of response compared to standard solutions. We also talk about how the framework works with tools that are already in place (like SIEM and SOAR) and keeps data private. The results indicate that employing GenAI can enhance cyber protection by making it more adaptable and intelligent. We conclude with an examination of the anticipated advantages, existing constraints (such as adversarial machine learning hazards and model interpretability), and prospective research avenues for the large-scale application of generative AI in cybersecurity. This study presents a multi-layered GenAI driven architecture that combines anomaly detection, LLM-based reasoning, and reinforcement learning to automate cybersecurity operations and minimize false positives.  © 2026 IEEE.
