SP  - 1159
EP  - 1166
DO  - 10.1109/ICCSDFAI70505.2026.11647893
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049064654?origin=resultslist
AB  - Ransomware has become one of the most sophisticated and rapidly evolving cyber threats, placing significant pressure on modern Security Operations Centers (SOCs). Analysts are required to examine massive amounts of Cyber Threat Intelligence (CTI) gathered from open-source feeds, technical reports, and threat-sharing platforms, yet much of this information is unstructured and difficult to process efficiently. This often increases analyst workload, slows incident response, and contributes to operational fatigue. To overcome these limitations, this study introduces MinervAI, an autonomous multi-agent CTI framework developed to automate ransomware intelligence analysis and support operational cyber defense activities. The proposed system combines LangChain ReAct agents, SecureBERT-based entity extraction, ransomware relevance detection, semantic MITRE ATT&CK mapping, and Large Language Model (LLM) reasoning within a unified intelligence pipeline. Using this architecture, the framework can automatically gather intelligence from multiple live threat sources, extract Indicators of Compromise (IoCs), identify ransomware-related activities, map attacker behaviors to MITRE ATT&CK techniques, and generate practical defense artifacts such as STIX 2.1 bundles, Sigma rules, YARA signatures, and Neo4j knowledge graphs. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves a ransomware classification accuracy of 97.99% while significantly reducing the manual effort required for CTI analysis. In addition, the complete pipeline operates with an average latency of only 47 seconds on commodity hardware without GPU acceleration, corresponding to nearly a 98% reduction in processing time compared to traditional analyst-driven workflows, while also providing full MITRE ATT&CK kill-chain coverage across all ten phases. © 2026 IEEE.
