TI  - SemGuard: A Hierarchical Shared Semantic Intelligence Architecture for Secure LLM Systems
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 IEEE World AI IoT Congress, AIIoT 2026
SP  - 382
EP  - 388
DO  - 10.1109/AIIoT68874.2026.11569137
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044090130?origin=resultslist
AB  - Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in healthcare, finance, legal services, and enterprise knowledge management, where they process highly sensitive data. However, security controls have not kept pace with this adoption. Unlike traditional software, LLMs expose an attack surface defined by natural language, making general defenses such as safety tuning, keyword blocklists, and generic classifiers insufficient for deployment-specific threats. This limitation is especially critical because the risks faced by medical, financial, and other enterprise systems differ fundamentally by domain. This paper presents SemGuard, a hierarchical shared-semantic intelligence architecture for secure LLM deployments. The architecture distributes security across localized and domain-specialized filter nodes, each backed by a vectorized knowledge base of representative threat seeds, and augments input sanitization with semantic similarity search by repurposing retrieval-augmented generation infrastructure for real-time threat detection. A shared intelligence layer accumulates blocked prompts across nodes, enabling collective learning and cross-domain threat coverage without manual retraining. We implement and evaluate SemGuard using open-source components, including n8n, ChromaDB, and both locally hosted and cloud-hosted LLMs, demonstrating a practical and deployable approach to adaptive LLM security. © 2026 IEEE.
