TI  - Identity and Access Management for Agentic AI: A Zero-Trust Security Framework
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Cybersecurity, Digital Forensics, and AI Applications, ICCSDFAI 2026
SP  - 1228
EP  - 1232
DO  - 10.1109/ICCSDFAI70505.2026.11648021
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105049057339?origin=resultslist
AB  - Agentic AI systems act autonomously across services, APIs, and data stores, creating identity and access management (IAM) challenges that traditional perimeter-based security frameworks do not address. Unlike human principals, AI agents spawn sub-agents dynamically, receive instructions from a language model vulnerable to prompt injection, and accumulate permissions through sequential tool calls. These properties invalidate the session-based, role-based access control model that existing IAM tools assume. This paper presents ZT-AgentIAM, a zero-trust security framework for managing AI agent identities and permissions at runtime. The framework defines four core components: dynamic token issuance with cryptographic scope binding, policy-based least-privilege enforcement via OPA Rego rules, behavioral attestation using an isolation forest anomaly detector, and continuous tamper-evident audit logging. We evaluate ZT-AgentIAM against five attack classes on a simulated AutoGen-based multi-agent orchestration environment. The framework achieves a mean attack detection rate of 93.4 percent across all threat categories with a full-pipeline latency overhead of 15.1 ms per request, confirming that zero-trust principles are operationally applicable to agentic AI deployments. © 2026 IEEE.
