TI  - A Graph-Orchestrated Agentic Framework for Real-Time Multimodal Email Threat Detection with Continual Learning using Large Language Models
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of the 4th International Conference on Inventive Computing and Informatics, ICICI 2026
SP  - 1611
EP  - 1618
DO  - 10.1109/ICICI68773.2026.11580636
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044530040?origin=resultslist
AB  - Email-based cyberattacks have grown considerably more dangerous, with adversaries now leveraging generative AI to craft convincing phishing messages that embed malicious URLs, deceptive images, QR codes, and forged header information to circumvent conventional security filters. Existing detection approaches depend predominantly on fixed, single-modality classifiers that process email components in isolation, rendering them ineffective against semantically complex and multi-layered attack strategies. This paper presents a Graph-Orchestrated Agentic Framework for autonomous and explainable email threat detection using Large Language Models. The proposed system employs a stateful multi-agent architecture coordinated through LangGraph, where a Header-URL Agent and a Text-Image Agent independently analyse their respective email modalities and forward structured risk signals to a central LLM-based meta-reasoner. The meta-reasoner synthesises all evidence into a final threat verdict accompanied by a human-readable explanation. A continual learning mechanism is incorporated to enable incremental adaptation to newly observed attack patterns without catastrophic forgetting. © 2026 IEEE.
