TI  - A Review of Large Language Models for Cybersecurity Defense: Core Capabilities, Application Practices, and Challenges
PY  - 2026
T2  - Proceedings of 3rd International Conference on Machine Intelligence and Digital Applications, MIDA 2026
SP  - 1999
EP  - 2009
DO  - 10.1145/3801438.3803160
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050650931?origin=resultslist
AB  - The rapid evolution of cyber threats - characterized by advanced persistent threats (APTs), software supply chain attacks, and AI-driven adversarial payloads - has exposed fundamental limitations in traditional rule-based and signature-based defense mechanisms. Large Language Models (LLMs), with core capabilities spanning natural language understanding, code engineering, logical reasoning, and autonomous decision-making, offer a transformative paradigm for cybersecurity defense. This paper presents a systematic review of LLM applications across the entire cybersecurity defense lifecycle. The review begins with an analysis of the core capabilities and key enabling technologies of LLMs - including prompt engineering, domain fine-tuning, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), and agent frameworks - that underpin their adaptation to security tasks. Application practices are then examined across four critical stages: pre-attack prevention and secure development (covering vulnerability detection, secure code generation, supply chain security, and malware/phishing detection), during-attack detection and threat awareness (including network intrusion detection, log analysis, and threat intelligence automation), post-attack response and disposition (encompassing digital forensics and automated incident response),. The paper further dissects the security risks inherent to LLMs themselves - such as prompt injection, jailbreaking, data poisoning, and model stealing attacks - along with corresponding defense technologies. Finally, key challenges are identified, including hallucination, real-time performance bottlenecks, domain knowledge scarcity, data silos, interpretability deficits, and compliance risks, with future research directions proposed toward building more robust, trustworthy, and human-centered intelligent defense systems. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).
