TI  - APT-Agent: A Training-Free, Unsupervised, LLM-Based Multi-agent Framework for Interpretable Advanced Persistent Threat Detection
PY  - 2026
T2  - Lecture Notes of the Institute for Computer Sciences, Social-Informatics and Telecommunications Engineering, LNICST
VL  - 686 LNICST
SP  - 107
EP  - 125
DO  - 10.1007/978-3-032-22545-0_7
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044796336?origin=resultslist
AB  - Advanced Persistent Threats (APTs)—noted for their stealth, targeting precision, and destructive impact—have become a major challenge in cybersecurity. With the progress of deep learning, deep-learning-based APT detection has emerged as the mainstream research direction. Nevertheless, current methods still face three key limitations: (1) dependence on large, labeled datasets containing attack samples and extensive domain knowledge; (2) prohibitive computational costs during training and inference, making real-world deployment difficult; and (3) a narrow focus on merely determining whether an attack exists, lacking interpretability and support for follow-up analysis. To overcome these issues, we introduce APT-Agent, the first training-free, unsupervised LLM-based multi-agent framework dedicated to APT detection. It coordinates three agents: (1) a Dispatcher Agent that decomposes natural-language requests into subtasks, dispatches them for execution, and aggregates the results into a unified response; (2) a Detection Agent that fuses LLM reasoning with Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) for efficient and accurate log detection; and (3) an Interpreter Agent that combines long-term memory with external tools to generate trustworthy explanations and mitigation advice. Evaluation results show that APT-Agent achieves outstanding detection performance across ten attack scenarios while incurring dramatically lower computational overhead than state-of-the-art APT detection methods. © ICST Institute for Computer Sciences, Social Informatics and Telecommunications Engineering 2026.
