TI  - Executable Risk Assessment: A Multi-Modal Approach to Autonomous Security Operations
PY  - 2026
T2  - 2026 International Conference on Data Science and Business Systems, ICDSBS 2026
DO  - 10.1109/ICDSBS69077.2026.11634799
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047949674?origin=resultslist
AB  - The so-called rapid proliferation of Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs) has contributed greatly to the complexity of vulnerability management in contemporary software ecosystems. Whereas the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) offers standard severity scores, the use of fixed scores is not sufficient to rely on in order to effectively prioritize remediation especially in automated security environments. Recent threat intelligence means that 29 per cent of vulnerabilities are exploited before or on the day of public disclosure, which argues the need to have context-sensitive, evidence-based prioritization systems. The paper describes an independent vulnerability remediation model that combines Evidence-Based Priority Scoring (EBPS), GNN-based reachability analysis, LLM-based patch synthesis, and Explainable AI (XAI). On the March 2026 Patch Tuesday dataset (82 CVEs), the framework achieves a high-priority triage F1-score of 0.83 (vs. 0.61 with CVSS-only) and reduces patch failure rates to 4.8 percent (compared to 1520 percent), and improves developer trust scores of 2.3 4.1/5 with XAI explanations. Comparative analysis with CVSS v4.0, Qualys VMDR, Tenable.io, and CVEDrill confirms that the proposed system is a complex improvement over the prioritization accuracy, reachability analysis, explainability, and remediation automation. © 2026 IEEE.
